# Ubuntu package list — Apple Silicon layer

`mkosi.images/apple-silicon/mkosi.conf` wires the Ubuntu-archive subset
below into the overlay build. Its finalize step prunes package payloads;
the liveiso aarch64 and sysupdate arm64 configs explicitly install the same
runtime packages. `mseries/test-image-wiring.py` checks this contract.
The safe Asahi DSP chain still needs image packaging (see below).

## Wired into the image (Ubuntu archive names)

- `pipewire-pulse`, `pipewire-alsa`, `rtkit` — see rationale in the
  previous revision of this file (commit history).
- `dbus-bin`, `libnotify-bin`, `pciutils`, `rfkill` — sleep notifications and gated Bluetooth diagnostics/recovery.
- `python3-gi` — the hardware helper's GObject bindings.
- `initramfs-tools` — present so `mkosi.postinst.chroot` can append the
  HID early-load modules to `/etc/initramfs-tools/modules`; pruned from
  the final overlay by `mkosi.finalize`.

The default YouTube menu/dock launcher and SVG are staged by `enhance.sh` into
`/usr/share`, retained by the factory overlay, and use an installed executable
rather than an owner's checkout. Both consumers install `bubblewrap`,
`xdg-dbus-proxy`, `desktop-file-utils` and `libgtk-3-bin`; final hooks refresh the
application/icon caches. The transformed install's first-boot job installs Brave;
factory images include the pinned private Brave runtime under `/usr/lib/mseries-brave`.
Its complete Ubuntu dependency list is in `video-decode/brave-factory.json`; all
three image configs carry it, and final hooks verify hashes and shared libraries.

## From the Ubuntu archive (names confirmed by archive membership, not tested)

- `pipewire-pulse` — PulseAudio-compat server (omarchy `audio.sh`: x86 gets
  this from the ISO; Apple Silicon has no ISO path, so it must be explicit)
- `pipewire-alsa` — ALSA compat for the same reason
- `rtkit` — realtime scheduling; only an optional dep of pipewire, without
  which the Asahi speaker DSP chain underruns (crackling under load)
- `linux-firmware-realtek` — firmware for the dock's Realtek USB Ethernet (`rtl_nic/rtl8153b-2.fw`); installed on the M1 Pro on 2026-10-14. Not yet wired into the image configs.
- `powertop` / `thermald` — power/thermal sanity on laptops (optional,
  evaluate during bring-up)

## Apps — Signal Desktop

Signal Desktop ships an official **arm64** build. Two transports, both wired:

- **Flatpak** (what the live elementary transform uses): `org.signal.Signal`
  from Flathub, aarch64-clean. Install with
  `flatpak install --user flathub org.signal.Signal`. elementary ships
  Flatpak + the Flathub/AppCenter remotes by default, so no extra source.
- **Official apt repo** (used by `transform/elementary-grow.sh` so the fresh
  root has a native `signal-desktop` package too):

  ```ini
  # /etc/apt/sources.list.d/signal-desktop.list
  deb [arch=arm64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/signal-desktop-keyring.gpg] \
      https://updates.signal.org/desktop/apt xenial main
  ```

  key: `https://updates.signal.org/desktop/apt/keys.asc` (dearmored).

The suite is literally `xenial` — Signal never renames it; do not "fix" it.

## DP Alt Mode kernel config (for a future Asahi-flavoured kernel)

USB-C dock video needs these **as modules** in the kernel config; they are
inert without the `fairydust` branch. See `USB-C-DOCK-DP-ALT-MODE.md`.

```
CONFIG_TYPEC=m
CONFIG_TYPEC_DP_ALTMODE=m
CONFIG_TYPEC_NVIDIA_ALTMODE=m
CONFIG_TYPEC_TBT_ALTMODE=m
```

## Safe speaker support wired into the factory image

`speakersafetyd` 1.1.2-4 from Ubuntu resolute/universe is installed in the
ARM64 Apple Silicon overlay, live installer and sysupdate images. The overlay
retains an enablement link for `multi-user.target`, so a fresh install starts
speaker protection at boot. This is the safety-only step; the DSP chain stays
out of the factory image until its first-boot volume behavior is qualified.

The package is built from Ubuntu source `rust-speakersafetyd` 1.1.2-4. Upstream
source is [AsahiLinux/speakersafetyd](https://github.com/AsahiLinux/speakersafetyd)
(MIT); the Ubuntu package remains installed from the signed Ubuntu archive, and
its copyright metadata is retained by dpkg. Ubuntu lists an ARM64 build; the
M1 Pro's transformed desktop has already run the matching speaker safety
service. No Apple hardware boot or audio behavior is inferred from image build.

`asahi-audio` 3.4-2 and `alsa-ucm-conf-asahi` 8-3 remain optional and unwired.
The transformed desktop's output test passed with the matched PipeWire LV2
plugin, but that does not qualify a first-boot factory DSP chain. Before adding
it, preserve the prior sink volume and keep the raw internal speaker hidden as
a fallback.

## Asahi-origin packages still missing a confirmed Ubuntu source

- `avd-fw` (Apple Video Decoder firmware `apple/avd-fw-v2-t0.bin`)
- `libva-v4l2_request-avd` (`asahi_drv_video.so` VA-API bridge)
- `asdcontrol` (Apple display brightness; omarchy-edge-only)

Evaluate Ubuntu Asahi Remix sources or pinned upstream build steps for these
remaining gaps. Do NOT point apt at Arch repositories.

## Kernel (open question, see BUILD-NOTES.md)

elementary `base` installs `linux-image-generic`. Whether resolute's generic
arm64 kernel carries the Apple Silicon drivers used here (`appledrm`,
`CONFIG_VIDEO_APPLE_AVD=m`, HID, NVMe, brcmfmac firmware paths) is unverified
for this factory image. The owner's current M1 Pro desktop instead runs
`7.1.13-1-2-fairydust-usb4-ARCH`; its successful boot does not validate
Ubuntu's generic kernel or our mkosi image. If not, the project needs either an
Asahi-flavoured kernel package or a custom kernel image — the largest single
work item.
